Lab 8: Deploying and Managing Custom Images in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Upload custom VM images to OpenStack Glance.
Use cloud-init for post-launch instance customization.
Create and manage image snapshots and versions.
Prerequisites
An active OpenStack environment with admin/user access.
OpenStack CLI (openstack client) installed and configured.
A Linux-based custom image (e.g., QCOW2 format) for testing.
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and OpenStack concepts.
Task 1: Upload Custom VM Images to Glance
Subtask 1.1: Prepare a Custom Image
Download a cloud-ready QCOW2 image (e.g., CentOS or Ubuntu cloud image):

wget https://cloud.centos.org/centos/9-stream/x86_64/images/CentOS-Stream-GenericCloud-9-latest.x86_64.qcow2
Replace the URL with the latest preferred image.
Verify the image file:

file CentOS-Stream-GenericCloud-9-latest.x86_64.qcow2
Expected Output:
CentOS-Stream-GenericCloud-9-latest.x86_64.qcow2: QEMU QCOW2 Image (v3), ...

Subtask 1.2: Upload Image to Glance
Upload the image using the OpenStack CLI:

openstack image create "CentOS-9-Stream" \
  --file CentOS-Stream-GenericCloud-9-latest.x86_64.qcow2 \
  --disk-format qcow2 \
  --container-format bare \
  --public
--public makes the image available to all projects (admin-only). Omit for private images.
Verify the upload:

openstack image list
Expected Outcome:
The image appears in the list with status active.

Troubleshooting Tip:
If the image status is queued or error, check disk format compatibility and Glance service logs.

Task 2: Use cloud-init for Post-Launch Customization
Subtask 2.1: Create a cloud-init Configuration File
Create a user-data file for initialization (e.g., cloud-init-config.yaml):
#cloud-config
hostname: mycustomvm
users:
  - name: demouser
    groups: sudo
    shell: /bin/bash
    sudo: ['ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL']
    ssh-authorized-keys:
      - ssh-rsa AAAAB3Nz... (your public key)
Replace the SSH key with your own.
Subtask 2.2: Launch an Instance with cloud-init
Launch an instance with the uploaded imageanduser-data`:

openstack server create --image CentOS-9-Stream \
  --flavor m1.small \
  --network private-net \
  --user-data cloud-init-config.yaml \
  my-custom-vm
Verify initialization:

openstack console log show my-custom-vm | grep "cloud-init"
Expected Outcome:
Logs show successful cloud-init execution (e.g., "Configuring ssh authorized keys").

Key Concept:
cloud-init automates hostname, user creation, and SSH key injection during boot.

Task 3: Create and Manage Image Snapshots and Versions
Subtask 3.1: Create a Snapshot from a Running Instance
Stop the instance (snapshots require a stopped VM for consistency):

openstack server stop my-custom-vm
Create a snapshot:

openstack server image create --name "snapshot-1" my-custom-vm
Verify the snapshot:

openstack image list --name "snapshot-1"
Subtask 3.2: Manage Image Versions
Upload a new version of the image (e.g., updated CentOS):

openstack image create "CentOS-9-Stream-v2" \
  --file /path/to/updated-image.qcow2 \
  --disk-format qcow2
Deprecate the old image:

openstack image set --property deprecation_reason="Replaced by v2" \
  --property deprecated=True CentOS-9-Stream
Expected Outcome:
The new image is active, and the old one is marked deprecated.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Uploaded a custom QCOW2 image to Glance.
Deployed an instance with cloud-init for automated configuration.
Created snapshots and managed image versions.
Next Steps:
Explore OpenStack Heat for orchestration or integrate image management with CI/CD pipelines.

Final Verification:
Run openstack image list and openstack server list to confirm all resources are correctly provisioned.
